1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:22,440
 [MUSIC PLAYING]

2
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:27,240
 In the case of Ghana, private sector led.

3
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:31,800
 The government was not aware, the government was not interested.

4
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:34,080
 And so the private sector took it up.

5
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:38,360
 But the private sector we're discussing is me.

6
00:00:38,360 --> 00:00:41,680
 I'm not exactly private sector, I'm academic.

7
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:44,960
 Only I did this job through the private sector.

8
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:48,480
 Meaning that I realized that you can wait for the university

9
00:00:48,480 --> 00:00:51,160
 forever, because they depend on government.

10
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:53,000
 And you can wait for the government forever.

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,060
 They have no clue.

12
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:58,600
 So I actually have been known to have said publicly

13
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:01,120
 that I cannot wait for the government

14
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:03,360
 to do it for the country.

15
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:05,160
 So I know how to do it.

16
00:01:05,160 --> 00:01:07,760
 I will do it, no matter how small it is,

17
00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:10,320
 just to make the point that we are able to do it.

18
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:13,320
 In so doing, maybe I can create an avalanche that

19
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:14,760
 will carry through the momentum.

20
00:01:14,760 --> 00:01:18,280
 And that is what I tried to do in the case of Ghana.

21
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:21,920
 Now on the continent as a whole, there are variety.

22
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:27,200
 Like in the case of Egypt, where the minister there

23
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:29,000
 happens to be one of our counterparts

24
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,160
 from internet society, ISOC, that's Tarek Kamel.

25
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:38,560
 And so there was a heavy government success built.

26
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:41,560
 In other places like South Africa,

27
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:44,360
 there was a good academic dose.

28
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:47,720
 So it has varied depending on the circumstance.

29
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:49,640
 But I think in much of West Africa,

30
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:53,360
 the thrust had been principally from private sector.

31
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:56,880
 Because I also went around helping Togo, private sector,

32
00:01:56,880 --> 00:01:58,520
 helping Gambia, private sector.

33
00:01:58,520 --> 00:02:01,200
 And so even Nigeria, giving them intellectual property

34
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:03,320
 so they could also begin to do it.

35
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:05,760
 So it became privacy helping private sector,

36
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:07,280
 but for the common good.

37
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:09,800
 Because we really felt that we had to go together.

38
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:12,640
 Otherwise, things would just disappear.

39
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:16,480
 I allowed some countries to transit their data through me.

40
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:18,560
 And Togo was an example.

41
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:21,320
 But in other places, I actually sent engineers

42
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:23,840
 to go and install nodes for them.

43
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:25,800
 And Gambia was an example.

44
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:29,440
 In other cases, I actually trained people like Swaziland.

45
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:32,840
 They brought the telco people to meet.

46
00:02:32,840 --> 00:02:33,920
 We trained.

47
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:37,280
 In other cases, we did consulting services

48
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:40,440
 to help Ethiopia, and so on and so forth.

49
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:42,400
 So that was roughly the progression,

50
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:44,640
 in that the names had to come first.

51
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:45,640
 But that was meaningful.

52
00:02:45,640 --> 00:02:47,200
 And we had to move mail.

53
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:48,640
 And so we had to do that.

54
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:52,160
 And then the next bit was get the capital cities connected.

55
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:53,720
 And that one was a mad rush.

56
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:56,320
 But it was a good community activity in the sense

57
00:02:56,320 --> 00:02:58,520
 that we wanted to help each other.

58
00:02:58,520 --> 00:03:00,080
 And that has continued till now.

59
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:03,840
 Because now, I actually run the African Network Operators Group.

60
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:06,880
 And the principal function of this group

61
00:03:06,880 --> 00:03:11,640
 is to sort of help operators support themselves.

62
00:03:11,640 --> 00:03:13,080
 And usually, we support ourselves

63
00:03:13,080 --> 00:03:16,480
 with building our capacity in many different areas,

64
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:19,720
 mostly in infrastructure-related things,

65
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:25,520
 like routing type of issues, or server services type of issues

66
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:29,040
 for those who want to build information resources.

67
00:03:29,040 --> 00:03:31,440
 The AFNUG has now become the meeting place

68
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:33,520
 for most of the technical community.

69
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:35,200
 And we meet only once a year.

70
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:37,320
 And when we meet, it's like for a period of two weeks,

71
00:03:37,320 --> 00:03:39,680
 within which we spend one week to train

72
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:42,960
 whoever has been admitted into the workshops.

73
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:46,000
 And we run four workshops in parallel now.

74
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:47,240
 It didn't start as four.

75
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:49,040
 It started as just two.

76
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:51,040
 And then we've added as we went along.

77
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:56,800
 We even have a French track in the routing side of things.

78
00:03:56,800 --> 00:03:59,680
 And when we meet after the workshop for the students,

79
00:03:59,680 --> 00:04:02,440
 then we try to sort of induct the students

80
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:04,920
 into what the real world is.

81
00:04:04,920 --> 00:04:07,840
 So we follow the workshop with meetings.

82
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:11,560
 And the meetings include the loss of parallel sessions.

83
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:14,040
 Those who are following the AFRINIC meetings

84
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:15,440
 will get to do that.

85
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:19,920
 Those who may be discussing some tutorials, maybe in security

86
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:21,480
 will also get to do that.

87
00:04:21,480 --> 00:04:25,240
 And then we also have a day for conference presentations

88
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:28,560
 of 20, 30 minutes from a variety of different areas.

89
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:30,760
 And so that has now become a major meeting

90
00:04:30,760 --> 00:04:32,600
 place for the engineers.

91
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:35,360
 And once a year, we all congregate.

92
00:04:35,360 --> 00:04:38,320
 And it's supposed to be service to community type of thing,

93
00:04:38,320 --> 00:04:40,160
 in that nobody gets paid.

94
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:43,800
 But we try to raise money for the student participants

95
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:44,800
 to come.

96
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:46,120
 And we've been getting funding.

97
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:48,800
 Cisco has been a regular funding source.

98
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:53,520
 We've got funding from IDRC, Internet Society, Francophonie,

99
00:04:53,520 --> 00:04:55,440
 and a whole number of others.

100
00:04:55,440 --> 00:04:59,080
 But over time, we're trying to change that program so that we

101
00:04:59,080 --> 00:05:01,480
 become more self-sustaining.

102
00:05:01,480 --> 00:05:06,440
 So participants who are coming in from, let's say, operators,

103
00:05:06,440 --> 00:05:10,960
 telcos, and so on, we insist that they pay more of their way

104
00:05:10,960 --> 00:05:14,160
 so that we can raise funding for academic and research people

105
00:05:14,160 --> 00:05:15,840
 to participate, and so on.

106
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:18,680
 And that was really how things have-- and it still

107
00:05:18,680 --> 00:05:23,080
 continues to be a major meeting point for many of our operators.

108
00:05:23,080 --> 00:05:25,920
 Of course, once we've got the connectivity in,

109
00:05:25,920 --> 00:05:27,880
 then there's a whole host of other issues

110
00:05:27,880 --> 00:05:30,080
 that began to show up.

111
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:32,160
 But we thought all of them can be solved

112
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:33,920
 within the same environment.

113
00:05:33,920 --> 00:05:36,840
 So you have CCTLD issues.

114
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:39,760
 Oh, you have AFTLD.

115
00:05:39,760 --> 00:05:42,080
 Let AFTLD focus that.

116
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:45,680
 You have Research and Educational Network Challenge.

117
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:48,560
 Oh, build a consensus around the same meeting place.

118
00:05:48,560 --> 00:05:53,040
 So to an extent, AFTLD has moved from being a workshop type

119
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:56,640
 of thing to a meeting place where many new things could

120
00:05:56,640 --> 00:05:59,760
 be incubated and then allowed to blossom and take

121
00:05:59,760 --> 00:06:00,720
 on their own life.

122
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:23,280
 [MUSIC PLAYING]

123
00:06:23,280 --> 00:06:25,840
 Now, the particular case about the NIC

124
00:06:25,840 --> 00:06:29,080
 was interesting in that it took us 10 years

125
00:06:29,080 --> 00:06:31,140
 to get to accreditation.

126
00:06:31,140 --> 00:06:34,640
 Of course, in the beginning, we certainly didn't know what it was.

127
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:36,360
 We knew it was very important.

128
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:38,600
 And for engineers, we knew that that

129
00:06:38,600 --> 00:06:41,920
 was what to focus on, none of the other things.

130
00:06:41,920 --> 00:06:44,920
 So we began to build consensus around it.

131
00:06:44,920 --> 00:06:50,080
 A proposal was made first in '95, then '97, a formal document.

132
00:06:50,080 --> 00:06:51,840
 And then we moved on.

133
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:55,560
 But we realized that we had to build something more than just

134
00:06:55,560 --> 00:06:56,640
 casual meetings.

135
00:06:56,640 --> 00:07:00,840
 So we went to deliberately to agree on specifics.

136
00:07:00,840 --> 00:07:05,200
 And the specifics was who becomes a member of the board.

137
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:09,080
 And we decided to do it geographically so that we

138
00:07:09,080 --> 00:07:11,320
 don't get too much caught into why is that there

139
00:07:11,320 --> 00:07:13,760
 are so many people from the north and nobody from the south.

140
00:07:13,760 --> 00:07:15,840
 So we said, OK, each one of the sub-regions

141
00:07:15,840 --> 00:07:18,040
 gets to elect one person.

142
00:07:18,040 --> 00:07:20,720
 So it's the members who will elect that person.

143
00:07:20,720 --> 00:07:23,720
 And that person will serve for a period of one, two, three

144
00:07:23,720 --> 00:07:25,720
 years, depending on the staggering.

145
00:07:25,720 --> 00:07:27,480
 And that is how that one also came.

146
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:32,280
 But the difficult thing with that was, as we were learning,

147
00:07:32,280 --> 00:07:34,600
 that meant more and more people were coming.

148
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:37,440
 So whenever we thought we had an agreement on something,

149
00:07:37,440 --> 00:07:40,320
 we were about to move forward in cooperation,

150
00:07:40,320 --> 00:07:41,440
 then more people come.

151
00:07:41,440 --> 00:07:43,160
 One case was very disturbing.

152
00:07:43,160 --> 00:07:46,280
 And that's sort of a moment.

153
00:07:46,280 --> 00:07:48,520
 And that one, John Puster, was also there.

154
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:49,640
 This was very early.

155
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:51,880
 And he had agreed that, me, just do it.

156
00:07:51,880 --> 00:07:52,680
 You have to do it.

157
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:54,160
 It has to happen.

158
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:57,840
 So I invited them all to a meeting with the African

159
00:07:57,840 --> 00:07:59,720
 engineers and everything.

160
00:07:59,720 --> 00:08:03,000
 And suddenly, somebody got up and said, no, no, no, no, no.

161
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:04,840
 Why don't we share it up into the countries

162
00:08:04,840 --> 00:08:07,400
 and have this all go our separate ways?

163
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:09,440
 And I mean, at that point, I thought

164
00:08:09,440 --> 00:08:11,440
 that was the end of the world.

165
00:08:11,440 --> 00:08:13,400
 And to be honest with you, I never

166
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:16,960
 felt so ashamed because I was a bit

167
00:08:16,960 --> 00:08:19,920
 of an interface between the African technical community

168
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:21,480
 and the global community.

169
00:08:21,480 --> 00:08:24,120
 I knew John Puster myself personally.

170
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:27,720
 And they've come to sort of help me.

171
00:08:27,720 --> 00:08:30,080
 But my people were not ready.

172
00:08:30,080 --> 00:08:34,960
 So all John could say to me was, get your people ready.

173
00:08:34,960 --> 00:08:36,440
 And you will have it.

174
00:08:36,440 --> 00:08:40,600
 So I had to continue until I was able to build some shared

175
00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:41,440
 consensus.

176
00:08:41,440 --> 00:08:44,600
 And in 2005, we got it.

177
00:08:44,600 --> 00:08:46,520
 And at that point, it was also evident

178
00:08:46,520 --> 00:08:50,600
 that if it has taken so long, there are maybe two factors

179
00:08:50,600 --> 00:08:52,080
 I have to consider.

180
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:56,200
 In 10 years, I would have upset a lot of people.

181
00:08:56,200 --> 00:08:58,360
 And this could be real in the sense

182
00:08:58,360 --> 00:09:01,080
 that the three registries had to give up

183
00:09:01,080 --> 00:09:03,920
 something for Afrinik to exist.

184
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:06,040
 So it's inevitable that I would have

185
00:09:06,040 --> 00:09:10,040
 made a number of people in those three registries uncomfortable.

186
00:09:10,040 --> 00:09:13,240
 So for me, it was obvious I could not

187
00:09:13,240 --> 00:09:15,600
 continue in the same capacity.

188
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:17,080
 Of course, I didn't tell my colleagues

189
00:09:17,080 --> 00:09:19,600
 that this was my real feeling.

190
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:25,080
 But it was also true that if I were to stay there,

191
00:09:25,080 --> 00:09:26,720
 I'll get less participation.

192
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:31,280
 I'll have less motivation for people to make an effort.

193
00:09:31,280 --> 00:09:33,520
 And what I needed more was more people

194
00:09:33,520 --> 00:09:37,760
 making efforts than just one person trying to do things.

195
00:09:37,760 --> 00:09:41,240
 Because I knew that the space was so large that even if I

196
00:09:41,240 --> 00:09:42,680
 had nothing to do with these things,

197
00:09:42,680 --> 00:09:44,960
 there was plenty for me to do.

198
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:49,360
 So the strategic thinking was, find a way

199
00:09:49,360 --> 00:09:52,560
 to exit without telling anybody.

200
00:09:52,560 --> 00:09:56,080
 Make sure you don't give out too much information for people

201
00:09:56,080 --> 00:09:58,440
 to capitalize and game on it.

202
00:09:58,440 --> 00:10:00,240
 So everybody thought I was indeed

203
00:10:00,240 --> 00:10:05,480
 going to be the next official chair, not the startup chair,

204
00:10:05,480 --> 00:10:07,080
 but the official chair.

205
00:10:07,080 --> 00:10:09,080
 And I let them think so.

206
00:10:09,080 --> 00:10:11,640
 But when it came to the board meeting,

207
00:10:11,640 --> 00:10:13,720
 I decided to dissolve the board.

208
00:10:13,720 --> 00:10:15,920
 And I got vote for it.

209
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:18,360
 And then I decided I would not run again.

210
00:10:18,360 --> 00:10:20,080
 And at the same time, certain people

211
00:10:20,080 --> 00:10:23,800
 who have been, you might say, controversial

212
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:27,800
 in their contribution, I tried to urge them not to.

213
00:10:27,800 --> 00:10:29,640
 And in some cases, I even told them

214
00:10:29,640 --> 00:10:32,880
 that if you tried, I would marshal the community

215
00:10:32,880 --> 00:10:34,240
 to vote you out.

216
00:10:34,240 --> 00:10:38,040
 And in some cases, they tried and they were voted out.

217
00:10:38,040 --> 00:10:42,480
 But that created a certain flow of maybe new entrants

218
00:10:42,480 --> 00:10:45,640
 wanting to contribute service to the community.

219
00:10:45,640 --> 00:10:47,480
 And that has continued till now.

220
00:10:47,480 --> 00:10:52,000
 And I think that that has been a very good thing for Afrinik.

221
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,840
 And of course, we also observed in the case of Afrinik

222
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:58,840
 that the ability to make local policy

223
00:10:58,840 --> 00:11:04,360
 does affect the effectiveness of the task at hand.

224
00:11:04,360 --> 00:11:09,680
 So for example, we know that soon after accreditation,

225
00:11:09,680 --> 00:11:14,280
 the type of membership we had began to skew more

226
00:11:14,280 --> 00:11:15,880
 towards a smaller size.

227
00:11:15,880 --> 00:11:17,800
 Because majority of the operators

228
00:11:17,800 --> 00:11:19,880
 were actually small operators.

229
00:11:19,880 --> 00:11:22,640
 And they were trying to fit into a large market,

230
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:24,640
 a large operator community environment.

231
00:11:24,640 --> 00:11:27,920
 And we were working with Riot and Afrinik and so on.

232
00:11:27,920 --> 00:11:29,960
 And that wasn't working so well.

233
00:11:29,960 --> 00:11:33,960
 So by us changing the policy to recognize the size,

234
00:11:33,960 --> 00:11:36,360
 the true size of our operators, we

235
00:11:36,360 --> 00:11:38,720
 were able to increase the membership.

236
00:11:38,720 --> 00:11:41,520
 And beyond that, we realized that our location also,

237
00:11:41,520 --> 00:11:44,240
 we changed the policy so that our location sizes were

238
00:11:44,240 --> 00:11:48,640
 smaller than the normal that a person, a typical operator,

239
00:11:48,640 --> 00:11:49,400
 would get.

240
00:11:49,400 --> 00:11:52,640
 So by knowing the community and making the policies,

241
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:56,120
 the community making the policy to suit itself,

242
00:11:56,120 --> 00:11:58,280
 you're able to get a better match

243
00:11:58,280 --> 00:12:00,040
 to the needs of the community.

244
00:12:00,040 --> 00:12:03,920
 And that has shown in the growth and uptake of, at least,

245
00:12:03,920 --> 00:12:07,480
 IPv4 addresses from Afrinik.

246
00:12:07,480 --> 00:12:10,440
 [MUSIC PLAYING]

247
00:12:14,240 --> 00:12:17,600
 [MUSIC PLAYING]

